<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · Work song · 1790 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ça Ira</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fr">Ça ira</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A cheerful song of the French Revolution from 1790. Its name means &quot;It'll be fine&quot;, words Benjamin Franklin liked to say.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Ladré (original words); later sans-culotte stanzas anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Jean-Antoine Bécourt</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French revolutionaries; the sans-culottes</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first heard in May 1790</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom · Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Ladré, a street singer (1790))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Ça ira&quot; (French for &quot;It'll be fine&quot;) was first heard in May 1790, in the early years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. Its words were written by Ladré, a former soldier who made his living as a street singer. The tune was a popular contredanse, &quot;Le carillon national&quot;, by Jean-Antoine Bécourt, a musician of the Théâtre Beaujolais; Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a> is said to have often played it on her harpsichord. The title phrase is said to come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, who was very popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> during his stay as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a>'s representative from 1776 to 1785 and reportedly answered questions about the American war with &quot;Ça ira, ça ira&quot; in his imperfect French.</p><p>The song first caught on as a work song during the preparations for the Fête de la Fédération of 1790, and it became an unofficial anthem of the revolutionaries. Its words changed several times, but every version kept the title words in the refrain. As the Revolution grew more radical, many sans-culottes sang far more aggressive stanzas that called for the nobility and the clergy to be lynched. In 1792 the warship La Couronne was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a> after the song.</p><p>The song outlived the Reign of Terror but was forbidden under the Directory in 1797. Its enemies took it up too: at the Battle of Famars in 1793, the British 14th Regiment of Foot attacked the French to its tune, and the regiment was later awarded it as a battle honour and quick march, a tradition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> Regiment keeps. It remained a song of protest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-schurz">Carl Schurz</a> recalled French exiles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> singing it, and dancing the Carmagnole, in anger at Louis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s coup of 1851, and Nikolay Chernyshevsky's novel What Is to Be Done? has its heroine sing a song with the refrain.</p><p>Composers quoted it, from Friedrich Witt in the 1790s to Nikolai Myaskovsky, who used it with the Carmagnole in his Symphony No. 6 (1923). Édith Piaf sang a sans-culotte-like version for Sacha Guitry's film Si <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> m'était conté (1954). The opening ceremony of the 2024 Summer Olympics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was titled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ca-ira">Ça Ira</a>, and there the metal band Gojira and the mezzo-soprano Marina Viotti performed a version from the windows of the Conciergerie, which won a Grammy Award for Best Metal Performance.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>In its first form, a confident, dancing refrain of optimism: whatever obstacles stand in the way, the Revolution will succeed. The later sans-culotte stanzas turn that confidence into open threats against aristocrats and priests.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Suivant la maxime de l’Évangile,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Following the maxim of the Gospel,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Du législateur tout s’accomplira.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All of the lawgiver's word will be fulfilled.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Celui qui s’élève, on l’abaissera ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He who raises himself, he shall be brought low;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Celui qui s’abaisse, l’on élèvera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He who lowers himself, he shall be raised up.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le vrai catéchisme nous instruira,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The true catechism will instruct us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et l’affreux fanatisme s’éteindra.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And dreadful fanaticism will die out.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour être à la loi docile,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To be obedient to the law,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tout Français s’exercera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Every Frenchman will train himself.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pierrette et Margot chantent à la guinguette ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pierrette and Margot sing at the tavern;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Réjouissons-nous, le bon temps viendra !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us rejoice, the good times will come!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le peuple français, jadis à quia ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The French people, once left without an answer;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’aristocrate dit mea culpa !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The aristocrat says mea culpa!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le clergé regrette le bien qu’il a ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The clergy mourns the wealth it has;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Par justice la nation l’aura.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By justice the nation will have it.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Par le prudent Lafayette,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Through the prudent Lafayette,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Tout trouble s’apaisera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All unrest will calm down.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 48 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Par les flambeaux de l’auguste assemblée,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>By the torches of the august assembly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le peuple armé toujours se gardera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The armed people will always keep guard over itself.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le vrai d’avec le faux l’on connaîtra ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The true from the false will be known;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le citoyen pour le bien soutiendra.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The citizen will stand up for the good.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">quand l’aristocrate protestera,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when the aristocrat protests,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le bon citoyen au nez lui rira,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The good citizen will laugh in his face,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sans avoir l’âme troublée,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Without having his soul troubled,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Toujours le plus fort sera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>[He] will always be the stronger.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Petits comme grands sont soldats dans l’âme,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Small and great alike are soldiers at heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pendant la guerre aucun ne trahira.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>During the war none will betray.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Avec cœur tout bon Français combattra ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With courage every good Frenchman will fight;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">S’il voit du louche, hardiment parlera.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If he sees something shady, he will speak out boldly.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Lafayette dit : &quot;Vienne qui voudra !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lafayette says: &quot;Come who will!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le patriotisme leur répondra ! &quot;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Patriotism will answer them!&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sans craindre ni feu ni flamme,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Without fearing either fire or flame,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le Français toujours vaincra !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Frenchman will always win!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ah ! ça ira, ça ira, ça ira !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ah! it will go, it will go, it will go!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Ladré (1790). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/%C3%87a_ira_(Ladr%C3%A9)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (14)</summary><ul><li>General (the refrain): 'ça ira', literally 'it will go', means 'it will work out, it will succeed'; translated literally each time it stands. This is Ladré's 1790 text, from the time of the Fête de la Fédération; the later, violent refrain ('les aristocrates à la lanterne') is not in it.</li><li>line 1.4: 'Du législateur tout s'accomplira', 'everything of the lawgiver will be fulfilled'. The lawgiver is most likely Christ, the giver of the Gospel's law (the next two lines paraphrase Luke 14:11); a reading as the National Assembly, the legislator of the day, is possible.</li><li>lines 1.5-1.6: 'l'on élèvera', literally 'one will raise'; rendered in the passive.</li><li>line 1.8: 'the true catechism' is set against 'fanaticism' (in the period's sense: religious bigotry).</li><li>line 2.2: Pierrette and Margot are stock names for working women; a 'guinguette' is a suburban open-air tavern with dancing.</li><li>line 2.5: 'à quia', a Latin tag: reduced to silence, unable to answer; the line has no verb in the original.</li><li>line 2.6: 'mea culpa' is Latin, 'through my fault', from the confession; kept as in the original.</li><li>lines 2.8-2.9: the Church's property was put at the nation's disposal in November 1789; 'regrette' here: is sorry for (losing) the wealth it has.</li><li>line 3.2: 'flambeaux', torches: the lights (enlightenment) of the National Assembly.</li><li>line 3.5: 'l'on connaîtra', literally 'one will know'; rendered in the passive.</li><li>line 3.8: lower-case 'quand' as in the source.</li><li>line 3.11: the original has no subject on this line ('Toujours le plus fort sera'); 'He' (the good citizen) is supplied.</li><li>line 4.5: 'Avec cœur', with heart, i.e. with courage.</li><li>line 4.6: 'du louche', something shady or suspicious.</li></ul></details></section>
